## Zinc-Based Metal–Organic Frameworks for HighPerformance Supercapacitor Electrodes: Mechanism Underlying Pore Generation
Heat treatment of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) has provided a wide variety of functional carbons coordinated with metal compounds. In this study, two kinds of zinc-based MOF (ZMOF), $\yen16$ (ZMOF1) and $\pmb{C_{8}}\mathsf{H_{4}O_{4}Z n}$ (ZMOF2), were prepared. ZMOF1 and ZMOF2 were carbonized at $\1000^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ forming CZMOF1 and CZMOF2, respectively. The specific su rface area $(S_{\mathbf{BET}})$ of CZMOF2 was $\sim2700\ m^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , much higher than that of CZMOF1 $(\sim1300\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1})$ . A supercapacitor electrode based on CZMOF2 achieved specific capacitances of 360, 278, and $221\textsf{F g}^{-1}$ at 50, 250, and $\mathbf{1000\mA\g^{-1}}$ in an aqueous electrolyte $(H_{2}S O_{4})$ , respectively, the highest values reported to date for ZMOF-derived electrodes under identical conditions. The practical applicability of the CZMOF-based supercapacitor was verified in non-aqueous electrolytes. The initial capacitance retention was $78\%$ after 100 000 charge/discharge cycles at $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . Crucially, the high capacitance of CZMOF2 arises from pore generation during carbonization. Below $\1000^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ pore generation is dominated by the $z_{\mathbf{\acute{n}}/\mathbf{\acute{C}}}$ ratio of ZMOFs, as carbon atoms reduce the zinc oxides formed during carbonization. Above $\1000^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ a high $\scriptstyle{\mathbf{o}}/{\mathbf{c}}$ ratio becomes essential for pore generation because the oxygen functional groups are pyrolyzed. These findings will provide insightful information for other metal-based MO Fderived multifunctional carbons.
1. Introduction Over the past several decades, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted increasing attention as a new alternative to carbon-based materials for energy storage devices.[1–4] High-capacity electrodes for lithium-ion batteries[5–8] and supercapacitors[9–14] have been prepared from various MOF-derived carbons. MOFs are one-, two-, or threedimensional porous crystalline structures composed of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands.[15] Their particle size and porous structures can be tuned to achieve ultrahigh porosity $(7140\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ .[16,17] The beneficial properties of carbon materials templated by MOFs have been reported. For instance, certain MOFs can retain their morphology and porous structures even after carbonization.[18,19] Moreover, several heteroatomdoped carbons have been prepared by MOF heating.[20–22] MOFs can be carbonized with metal atoms such as Fe, Co, or Ni, yielding carbons with a certain degree of graphitization.[23–26] Doping with other metals such as Mg, Al, or Zn obtains amorphous carith metal clusters or metal oxides,[20–23,27–39] which can then vceardbion h.[y2d4,r2o5,fl2u8,o2r9i] (AHmF)onogr thyed dcihffleoreicnt HMClO)Fas,c Ztno-byaiseld MOFs (ZMOFs) are of particular interest because they allow easy fabrication of porous carbons and exhibit a considerably large specific surface area.[30,33–35,37–39] In this fabrication, ZnO is reduced to Zn by the surrounding carbon, and the remaining $Z\mathrm{n}$ sublimates at relatively low temperature (boiling point: $908~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ .[32,33,35,39] The activation process attains high porosity in a single calcination step, avoiding the need for strong and hazardous acids such as HF or HCl. ZMOFs are expected as next-generation carbon precursors for porous because they can be easily fabricated and have a large surface area. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ of ZMOF-derived carbons is linearly related to the $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{C}$ ratio of the precursors.[30,31] Therefore, the conversion of $Z\mathrm{n}$ compounds might be related to the dynamic behavior of $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ and its effect on both pore formation and reduction with subsequent metal sublimation. Hence, we must clarify the relationtween the dynamic behavior of $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ and the pore formation during carbonization.
The present study aims to elucidate the pore generation mechanism in ZMOFs during carbonization. To this end, we synthesized two types of ZMOFs with different $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C$ ratios (denoted as ZMOF1 and ZMOF2). The corresponding ZMOFs were calcined at $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a nitrogen flow (Figure 1). The resulting carbons (referred to as CZMOFs) from ZMOF1 and ZMOF2 are denoted as CZMOF1 and CZMOF2, respectively. Furthermore, we revealed the effect of $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{C}$ ratio and oxygen on pore generation. The phase transformation and pore formation during carbonization were studied using in situ techniques, which provide high-resolution measurements of the bulk properties of samples. The samples were characterized by several dynamic techniques, such as in situ X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis. To evaluate the potentials of the CZMOFs as electrode materials with aqueous electrolytes, we correlated the structural information with the electrochemical properties. Although the supercapacitor electrodes of MOF-derived carbons have been investigated, the practical applicability of MOF-derived supercapacitors appears to be sparsely reported. Thus, we assembled supercapacitor cells with non-aqueous electrolytes to demonstrate the practical applicability of the CZMOF-based electrode materials. The durabilities of the CZMOFs were then verified in 100 000 charge/discharge cycles. We attempted to confirm whether we can extend the lifetime and range of applicable temperatures of ZMOF electrodes.
