## 2.4. Applicability of CZMOFs as Supercapacitor Electrodes
The electrochemical properties of the CZMOF1 and CZMOF2 supercapacitor electrode materials were tested to demonstrate their feasibility in energy storage devices. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of both CZMOFs exhibited their capacitive behavior (Figure S3a, b, Supporting Information). Figure 5a and Table S4, Supporting Information show the relationship between the specific voltammetric charge $\left(\mathrm{Q}/\mathrm{C}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\right)$ ) and $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ of the four carbon samples (CZMOF1, CZMOF2, YP50F, and MSP20). Q has a linear relation with $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ at a moderately lower sweep rate (1, 5, and $10~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ ). The results mean that $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ can provide a good correlation with the induced $\mathsf{Q}$ based on a pure electric double layer when using aqueous electrolytes $\mathrm{(H_{2}S O_{4})}$ . This is because $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ was calculated based on the monolayer of the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed on the outmost surface of a sample.[45] The Nyquist plots of both samples exhibited vertical lines in the low-frequency region, confirming their ideal capacitive behavior,[47] and their frequency range was 1.0 MHz to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ (Figure 5b). In the high-frequency region, semicircles were observed in both spectra, indicating that resistances were introduced by the inter-particle gaps, the particle/current collector interfaces, and charge transfer.[48] The total resistance of the CZMOF2 electrolytic cell was higher than that of CZMOF1. To provide a conceivable explanation of the difference, an equivalent circuit model (inset of Figure 5 b) was used to separate the individual resistances, such as the solution resistance $\mathrm{(R_{1})}$ , contact resistance owing to particle gaps or the particle/current collector interface $\left(\mathsf{R}_{2}\right)$ , and diffusion resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{3}\right)$ , from the total cell resistance. $\mathbf{M}_{3}$ indicates the restricted linear diffusion model.[49] $\mathtt{R}_{1}$ and ${\tt R}_{2}$ were not so different for the two CZMOF-based cells, whereas $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{d}3}$ of CZMOF2 was much higher than that of CZMOF1, as shown in Table S5, Supporting Information. This is owing to the hierarchical porous structures of CZMOF2, as they do not allow electrolyte ions to deeply access pores, increasing the diffusion time $\left(\mathrm{t}_{\mathrm{d}3}\right)$ .
