## 2.5. The Practical Applicability of CZMOF Electrodes in Supercapacitor Cells
Non-aqueous electrolytes are used in practical supercapacitors because they offer a wider voltage window and provide higher energy density than aqueous electrolytes[50] A $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ solution of tetraethylammonium tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{(TEABF_{4,})}$ ) in propylene carbonate (PC) can withstand a higher voltage (\~2.7 V)[51] compared with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ $(\sim1.2~\mathrm{V})$ . Thus, to demonstrate the practical feasibility of the CZMOF-based supercapacitors, we constructed supercapacitor cells containing the CZMOF electrodes in $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}/P C}$ electrolyte. Tests were performed in a two-electrode system. Figure 6a,b shows the GCD curves of the CZMOF1 and CZMOF2 samples at several current densities (0.5, 1.0, 2.0, and $5.0\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ ). Both samples demonstrated typical capacitive behavior. The specific capacitances of CZMOFs, YP50F, and MSP20 are compared in Figure S4 and Table S6, Supporting Information. The specific capacitance was lower in $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}}$ than in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ for all samples (Table S6, Supporting Information). This difference was attributable to the different sizes of the electrolytes. The estimated diameters of solvated $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}$ were 0.56 and $0.76~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively,[50] whereas those of $\mathrm{TEA}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{BF}^{4-}$ were reported as 1.36 and $1.40~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.[50] As shown in the Ragone plots of CZMOF1 and CZMOF2 (Figure 6c), the energy density $(\mathrm{Wh~kg}^{-1}\cdot$ ) was higher in $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}/P C}$ than in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , because the withstanding voltage was higher in the organic electrolyte $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}/P C}$ than in inorganic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . The conductivity is reportedly lowered in non-aqueous solutions because the solvated anions and cations are larger and the viscosity is higher than in aqueous solutions.[52] In the present test, the energy densities of CZMOF1 and CZMOF2 were lower in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ than in $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}}$ at high power densities (over $5\mathrm{~kW~kg}^{-1}\cdot$ ). This result can be explained by the greater thickness (and hence resistance) of the pellet electrode than the thin film electrode ${\sim}800~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ vs. $\mathord{\sim}50~\upmu\mathrm{m}\$ ) (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Figure 6d shows the results of GCD tests conducted at 1.0 A ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}}^{-1}$ over 300 cycles at 25, 50, and $75~^{\circ}C$ during 1–100, 101– 200, and 201–300 cycles, respectively, for the two CZMOFs. The corresponding capacitances are shown in Table S7, Supporting Information. In both samples, the capacitance increased with temperature because high temperatures confer low viscosity and high conductivity.[52] However, the capacitance retention of CZMOF2 decreased
