## 2.6. Mechanism Underlying Pore Generation
The high performance of the above-mentioned supercapacitor electrodes was attributable to the highly porous CZMOFs. We now investigate the effects of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and O on pore formation and hence elucidate the mechanism underlying pore generation in ZMOFs during carbonization.
To investigate the effect of $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{C}$ ratio on pore generation, we explored the dynamic behavior of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms in the carbon frameworks at high temperatures $(500-1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ .
## 3. Conclusion
Two CZMOFs were successfully synthesized by activating ZMOFs with different $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C$ ratios (0.0625 and 0.1250) and $\mathrm{{{O/C}}}$ ratios (0.25 and 0.50). The $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{C}$ ratio significantly affected the specific surface areas of the CZMOFs below $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The in situ XRD analysis of the ZMOFs provided fundamental information on the dynamic behaviors of $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ in the carbonized ZMOFs, revealing that the $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C$ ratio also affected the stability of crystalline $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ at gh temperatures. Furthermore, numerous pores were developed dur  
ing $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ reduction and $Z\mathrm{n}$ sublimation up to $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A high O/C   
ratio was essential for pore generation at ${>}1000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ because pores were nerated by pyrolysis of oxygen functional groups. Whether oxygen   
atoms are absent or present in the MOF structure, oxide formation and   
decomposition of oxygen functional groups during the carbonization   
process play a significant role in pore control. This finding is helpful for e pore control of the carbons derived from other metal-based MOF.
CZMOF2 with an $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ of $2678\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ exhibited specific capacitances of 360, 278, and $221\textup{F g}^{-1}$ at current densities of 50, 250, and $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. To date, these capacitances are the highest reported values in supercapacitors with $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF-derived electrodes and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte. Importantly, the two symmetric supercapacitor cells based on CZMOF1 and CZMOF2 with $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}}$ electrolyte exhibited excellent performance, indicating their applicability as supercapacitor electrode materials.
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of ZMOFs: ZMOF1 was synthesized from two types of solutions: (A) zinc acetate (Nacalai Tesque Inc., Japan) $(176~\mathrm{mg})$ dissolved in $30-\mathsf{m L}$ N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) (Nacalai Tesque Inc., Japan) and (B) 4,4-stilbene dicarboxylic acid (Tokyo Chemical Industry Co. Ltd., Japan) $(858~\mathrm{mg})$ dissolved in $400~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of NMP. Subsequently, solution A was transferred to solution B by a liquid delivery pump (flow speed $\sim4.0\mathrm{\mL\min^{-1}}.$ . The mixture was left for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $40~^{\circ}C.$ The formed precipitate was washed with $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of NMP and dried at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ under a vacuum for $12{\mathsf{h}},$ yielding a powdery product $(\sim220\ \mathrm{mg})$ . ZMOF2 was similarly synthesized from two types of solutions: (A) as described above and (C) terephthalic acid (Tokyo Chemical Industry Co. Ltd., Japan) $(532\ \mathrm{mg})$ dissolved in $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of NMP. Solution A was transferred to solution C by a liquid delivery pump (flow speed $\sim4.0\mathrm{\mL\min^{-1}}.$ ). The subsequent steps were as described for ZMOF1. The weight of the final powdery product was $\sim230~\mathsf{m g}.$ Similar MOF synthesis procedures are reported elsewhere.[40–42]
Preparation of CZMOFs: The ZMOF powder was placed in a ceramic boat made of alumina; the boat was transferred to a quartz furnace tube. The sample was heated under nitrogen, with the purity of $99.99\%,$ at a flow of $200~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and a heating rate of $10\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}\ {\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ until the temperature reached $1000^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ This temperature was maintained for ${\mathsf{5h}},$ following which the samples were naturally cooled to room temperature $(18-25~^{\circ}C)$ . A black powder was collected (CZMOFs). The process was repeated several times, and the mixture of the samples was characterized as follows. Four batches of each of the CZMOFs samples (CZMOF1_b1–b4 and CZMOF2_b1–b4) were prepared for reproducibility (Table S2, Supporting Information). CZMOF1_b3 and CZMOF2_b1 were used for all the characterizations. To explore the relationship between the surface area of the calcined ZMOFs and the heating profile (Figure 7b), ZMOF1 and ZMOF2 were calcined at a flow rate of $200~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and a heating rate of $10\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}\ {\mathsf{m i n}}^{-1}$ up to a corresponding temperatures of 500, 700, 900, and $1000^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and to 300, 500, 700, 900, and $1000^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ respectively. Then, the samples were naturally cooled to room temperature $(18-25~^{\circ}C)$ . Besides, ZMOF1 and ZMOF2 were calcined until the temperature reached $1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under the same conditions described above, and the temperature was maintained for several hours $\cdot1,3,$ and $5\mathfrak{h}$ ). The CZMOF1 and CZMOF2 obtained here, which were calcined at $1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for ${\mathsf{5h}},$ are denoted as CZMOF1_b2 and CZMOF2_b3 in Table S2, Supporting Information, respectively.
Fabrication of electrodes (for aqueous electrolytic cells): The active material ( $\cdot30~\mathrm{mg}$ of CZMOF1 or CZMOF2), a conductive additive (DENKA BLACK, Denka Co. Ltd., Japan) $(3.75~\mathsf{m g})$ and a poly (tetrafluoroethylene) (PTFE) binder (6-J, Chemours-Mitsui Fluoroproducts Co. Ltd., Japan) $(3.75~\mathsf{m g})$ were mixed at a mass ratio of 8:1:1 and kneaded with an agate mortar and pestle until a sheet material was formed (see Figure S18a,b, Supporting Information). Disks of diameter $13~\mathsf{m m}$ were prepared by molding and compressing $(60~M\mathsf{P a})$ the sheet material in a powder-compressed tablet machine (Shimadzu Corporation, Japan) (see Figure S18c,d, Supporting Information). The working electrode was obtained by attaching the disk to a titanium (Ti) mesh current collector, which was not dissolved in ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ (Figure S19, Supporting Information). The electrochemical properties of the Zn-activated carbons (CZMOF1 and CZMOF2) were compared with those of the reference activated carbons, namely YP50F (coconut shell origin, physical activation with steam; Kuraray Chemical Co. Ltd., Japan) and MSP20 (phenol resin origin, chemical activation with KOH; Kansai Coke and Chemicals, Japan) used for the production of porous carbons and supercapacitor electrode materials.[44] The same fabrication process was applied to YP50F and MSP20. The mass of the active material in each electrode disk was $37.5~\mathrm{mg}.$ Although mass loading increases the total resistance of the supercapacitor electrodes, it is required for avoiding mass error and evaluating the exact capacitance.
Fabrication of electrodes (for supercapacitor cells with non-aqueous electrolytes): Supercapacitor cells were prepared from electrode films of the CZMOFs. The films were deliberately thin to reduce the resistance, which increases with film thickness. An active material, carbon black (DENKA BLACK, Denka Company Limited, Japan), and a binder (W#9300, Kureha Corporation, Japan) were mixed at a mass ratio of 8:1:1. The mixture was stirred in NMP for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The obtained slurry was pasted onto an etched aluminum foil and was evacuated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to remove NMP from the carbon. The two activated carbons (YP50F and MSP20) were used as the reference electrodes in supercapacitor cells with non-aqueous electrolytes.
Material characterization: The morphology of each sample was characterized by SEM (JSM-6010LA, JEOL Ltd., Japan) at an accelerating voltage of $15~\mathsf{k V}$ (SE mode) and by TEM (JEM-2100F, JEOL Ltd., Japan) at an accelerating voltage of $200~\mathsf{k V}.$ In preparation for SEM, the powdery sample was set onto a carbon tape on an Al sample holder $(10\ \mathrm{mm}\ (\mathrm{\varphi})\times10\ \mathrm{mm}$ (H)). For TEM, the sample dispersed in ethanol was poured onto a holey carbon film supported on $3-\mathsf{m m}$ Cu grids, followed by drying at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ . The mass and molar ratios of each sample were examined through EDX spectroscopy (JEM-2100F, JEOL Ltd., Japan) and determined from the crystallographic information files.[40,42] The crystallinities of the resulting products were confirmed in a powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis (Smart Lab SE, Rigaku, Japan) with Cu $\mathsf{K}\upalpha_{1}$ ${\Lambda=0.154~\mathrm{nm}}$ radiation at a scan rate of $10^{\circ}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ . The in situ PXRD patterns of the corresponding ZMOFs were collected during heating to $1100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in $50~^{\circ}C$ increments under a nitrogen flow of $200~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The reference materials were commercial $z_{\mathsf{n O}}$ particles (purity $99.0\%;$ Nacalai Tesque Inc., Japan) and commercial ZIF-8 (Z1200, SigmaAldrich, USA). The graphitization degree was investigated by Raman spectroscopy (Raman, NRS-4500NMDS, JASCO, Japan) with a 532-nm excitation laser calibrated on Si. The intensity and exposure time were $2{\mathrm{~mw}}$ and $300~\mathsf{s},$ respectively. The surface functionalities of the samples were examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XP, ESCA5700, ULVAC-PHI Inc., Japan) with $M g\mathsf{K}\upalpha_{1}$ radiation at an accelerating voltage of $15{\mathsf{k V}}.$
The phase-change temperature of each sample was determined by TG–DTA (DTG-60, Shimadzu Corporation, Japan), which was performed at $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ under a nitrogen flow of $200~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . In preparation for TG–DTA, ${\sim}5~\mathsf{m g}$ of the powdery sample was placed in the Pt holder. Nitrogen adsorption/desorption (BEL-SORP min, Microtrac-BEL, Japan) isotherms were acquired at $77~\mathsf{K}$ for the $S_{B E T}$ measurement, average pore size distribution, and information on the pore structure of each sample. Prior to the measurements, the samples were degasified by heating at $300^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $24\ h$ in a vacuum. The $S_{B E T}$ of each sample was estimated from the isotherms by the BET method $(P/P_{0}=0.05–0.10)$ . The $S_{\mathrm{{meso}}}$ was estimated by the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) method. Total pore $(V_{\mathrm{total}}),$ micropore $(\sim2\ \mathsf{n m})$ $(V_{\mathrm{micro}})_{\mathrm{\smallm}}$ and mesopore $(2-50\ \mathsf{n m})$ $(V_{\mathrm{meso}})$ volumes were calculated by the BET, MP (micropore analysis method), and BJH methods, respectively. The PSD was calculated using non-local density functional theory (NLDFT).
Electrochemical measurements (for aqueous electrolytic cells): The electrochemical properties of the supercapacitor electrodes based on porous carbons were measured in a three-electrode system (with reference, counter, and working electrodes of $\mathsf{\mathsf{A g/A g C l}},$ platinum, and a porous carbon, respectively). The electrolyte solution was 1.0 M ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ (Nacalai Tesque Inc., Japan). Prior to measurement, the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ in the electrolytic cell was bubbled with ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ gas for $30\mathrm{\min}$ to remove the dissolved oxygen. CV, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), and
